course: dictionary
lesson: 7139
title: A pensioner is someone who gets a pension
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pensioner is a person who gets a pension. A pension is regular money paid to someone after they stop working, usually because they are old. «Pensioners pay half price» tells you that pensioners often get discounts, so they probably have less money than people who still work.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «A kind pensioner next door helped the children carry their shopping.» The words next door and helped tell you a pensioner is a person who lives nearby, probably an older person. Ask: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Be careful about where you are. In the UK, Australia and Ireland, pensioner often means any older person. In the USA people usually say retiree or senior citizen instead. Sometimes the word is used playfully for someone acting old. Context and a dictionary help you choose the right meaning.
fact: A pensioner is a person who gets a pension.
fact: A pension is regular money paid after you stop working, usually because you are old.
fact: «Old-age pensioner» (OAP) is a phrase used in the UK, Australia and Ireland.
fact: In the USA, people often say retiree or senior citizen instead of pensioner.
fact: As an informal joke, a pensioner can be a cheeky word for someone acting old.
exercise: «Pensioners pay half price.» What does pensioner mean in this sentence? | A person who is always late | *A person who gets a pension, usually an older person | A child who goes to school | Shop worker who sells tickets
exercise: Which phrase is used in the UK for an older person who gets a pension? | Winter walker | *Old-age pensioner | Young explorer | Fixed-speed driver
exercise: Which word would an American more likely use instead of pensioner? | Teenager | Pedestrian | Janitor | *Retiree
test: What is a pension? | *Regular money paid after you stop working | A school holiday | A bus ticket | A type of cake
test: «A kind pensioner next door helped the children.» Where does the pensioner probably live? | *Nearby, next door to the children | At the top of a mountain | Far away on a ship | Inside the school
exam: «On a fixed income» means having little money, usually as a pensioner. Which best describes a pensioner on a fixed income? | Works every day in a busy office | Has no home and no family | Has more money than anyone else | *Has little money, usually from a pension
